# Instruction cheatsheet

This page is for a reader who writes a [rung](glossary.md#rung) and looks up the form of
an [instruction](glossary.md#instruction). It gives the 40 instructions and the 182
[conversions](glossary.md#conversion) of logex on 1 page. Each card gives the
[operands](glossary.md#operand) and what the instruction does on a
[true rung](glossary.md#true-rung) and on a [false rung](glossary.md#false-rung). Each
card links to the page with the full rules.

> #### Warning {: .warning}
>
> Do not use logex for a [safety function](glossary.md#safety-function). logex is not a
> safety controller. The emergency stop that cuts the power of a machine must work with
> no part of logex. [What logex does not have](not-supported.md) gives each limit.

The cards use these letters:

- `a` and `b`: the values that an instruction reads. A value is a [tag](glossary.md#tag),
  a member, an element, a bit or a [literal](glossary.md#literal), as the instruction
  lets.
- `d`: the tag, member, element or bit that an instruction writes.
- `s` and `p`: the storage bit and the pulse bit of a one-shot.
- `t`: a timer. `c`: a counter. `f`: an instance of a function block.
- `n`: a number of bits. `l`: a label.

Power in is the [power flow](glossary.md#power-flow) to an instruction. Power out is the
power flow that the instruction gives to the next instruction. A run of an instruction is
1 time that a [scan](glossary.md#scan) does the instruction. A scan does not do the
instructions of a rung that a [jump](glossary.md#jump) skips.

## Rungs and operands

### Series and branches

<!-- unchecked: 3 forms of a rung, each compiled with a, b and d declared bool -->
```text
xic a xic b ote d
( xic a | xic b ) ote d
( xic a | ( xic b | xio a ) ) ote d
```

| Item | Rule |
|---|---|
| Instructions in series | Line 1: `d` is 1 if `a` and `b` are 1. |
| A [branch](glossary.md#branch) | Line 2: `d` is 1 if `a` or `b` is 1. A branch has 1 [leg](glossary.md#leg) or more. A leg can hold a branch, as in line 3. |
| Each leg | logex runs every leg, from the first leg to the last, also after an earlier leg passes power. Thus an `ote` in a later leg writes in each scan. |
| A later leg | It reads the values that an earlier leg writes in the same scan. |
| The end of a rung | A newline. A rung is 1 line. |
| A rung that writes nothing | An error. A rung must write a tag, a member or a bit, run an instance, keep a storage bit or jump. A rung of 1 `lbl` alone is correct if a `jmp` names the label. |
| `XIC`, `Xic` | The same [mnemonic](glossary.md#mnemonic) as `xic`. A mnemonic is a [reserved word](glossary.md#reserved-word). |

[Source syntax](source-syntax.md) gives each rule of a line, a rung and a branch.

### An example

This [program](glossary.md#program) has a [seal-in](glossary.md#seal-in), a [timer](glossary.md#timer), a
[counter](glossary.md#counter) and a comparison.

<!-- file: cheatsheet.ld -->
```text
var_input start bool
var_input stop bool
var_input eye bool
var_output motor bool
var_output full bool
var delay ton
var boxes ctu

( xic start | xic motor ) xio stop ote motor
xic motor ton delay 2000
xic delay.dn xic eye ctu boxes 12
ge boxes.cv 10 ote full
```

<!-- rung: cheatsheet.ld -->
![The 4 rungs of cheatsheet.ld as a ladder drawing. Rung 1: a branch with the contacts start and motor, then the xio contact stop, then the coil motor. Rung 2: the contact motor, then a box ton with the name delay and the preset 2000. Rung 3: the contacts delay.dn and eye, then a box ctu with the name boxes and the preset 12. Rung 4: a box ge with boxes.cv and 10, then the coil full.](../assets/cheatsheet.svg)

In the drawing, a [contact](glossary.md#contact) is 2 vertical lines. The contact of
`xio` also has a slash. A [coil](glossary.md#coil) is 2 arcs. `ton`, `ctu` and `ge` are
boxes.

### Operands

| Operand | Example |
|---|---|
| A tag | `motor` |
| A [member](glossary.md#member) | `delay.dn`, `boxes.cv` |
| An [element](glossary.md#element) | `lamps[2]` |
| An element, its subscript in a tag | `lamps[slot]` |
| A [bit](glossary.md#bit) | `status.3` |
| A bit, its number in a tag | `status.[slot]` |
| An integer literal | `12`, `-120`, `30_000`, `16#ff`, `2#0101` |
| A real literal | `60.0`, `-0.25`, `1.5e3` |

A member is a value of an [instance](glossary.md#instance). An element is 1 value of an
[array](glossary.md#array). A bit is 1 bit of an integer or of a
[bit string](glossary.md#bit-string).

If the [host](glossary.md#host) gives a [state](glossary.md#state) with no value for a
tag, the tag reads as 0. [Source syntax](source-syntax.md#names) gives each form of a
name. [Literals](literals.md) gives each form of a number.

## Contacts and coils
{: .col-2}

### Contacts

| Text | Passes power if | Operand |
|---|---|---|
| `xic a` | `a` is 1 | A bool |
| `xio a` | `a` is 0 | A bool |

A contact writes nothing.

### Coils

| Text | True rung | False rung |
|---|---|---|
| `ote d` | Writes 1 | Writes 0 |
| `otl d` | Writes 1 | Writes nothing |
| `otu d` | Writes 0 | Writes nothing |

`d` is a bool. A coil passes on its power in, so more instructions can come after it.
No instruction writes a `var_input` tag. `otl` is the [latch](glossary.md#latch) and
`otu` is the [unlatch](glossary.md#unlatch).

[Instructions: contacts and coils](instructions-contacts-and-coils.md) gives the full
rules.

## One-shots

### `ons`, `osr` and `osf`

<!-- unchecked: the forms of the 3 one-shots -->
```text
ons s
osr s p
osf s p
```

| Mnemonic | What it does | Power out |
|---|---|---|
| `ons` | Passes power for 1 scan when its power in goes from false to true | True in that scan only |
| `osr` | Writes 1 to `p` for 1 scan when its power in goes from false to true. Writes 0 to `p` in each other run. | Its power in |
| `osf` | Writes 1 to `p` for 1 scan when its power in goes from true to false. Writes 0 to `p` in each other run. | Its power in |

`s` is the [storage bit](glossary.md#storage-bit) and `p` is the
[pulse bit](glossary.md#pulse-bit). Each is a bool that the instruction writes.

### Rules of the one-shots

| Item | Rule |
|---|---|
| The [first scan](glossary.md#first-scan) | No [rising edge](glossary.md#rising-edge) and no [falling edge](glossary.md#falling-edge). The instruction gives no pulse. |
| An [online edit](glossary.md#online-edit) | An edit that adds the instruction or changes its rung blocks it 1 time. It gives no pulse in the next scan that runs it. [Online edit](online-edit.md#one-shots-and-pulse-coils) gives each case. |
| The storage bit | It holds the power in of the last run. 2 [one-shots](glossary.md#one-shot) cannot have the same storage bit. `s` cannot also be `p`. |
| Another instruction that writes `s` | A [warning](glossary.md#warning) |
| Another instruction that writes `p` | A warning |
| An element or a bit as `s` or `p` | It takes a literal [subscript](glossary.md#subscript) or bit number, not a tag: `lamps[2]`, not `lamps[slot]`. |
| A rung that a jump skips | The one-shot does not change. In its next run, it compares its power in with the power in of its last run. |

[Instructions: contacts and coils](instructions-contacts-and-coils.md#one-shots) gives
the full rules.

## Timers

### `ton`, `tof`, `tp` and `rto`

<!-- unchecked: the forms of the 4 timers, each with the preset 3000 ms -->
```text
ton t 3000
tof t 3000
tp t 3000
rto t 3000
```

| Mnemonic | `dn` is 1 | `tt` is 1 |
|---|---|---|
| `ton`, on-delay | After the rung is true for 3000 ms, until the rung goes false | While the rung is true and `dn` is 0 |
| `tof`, off-delay | While the rung is true, and for 3000 ms after it goes false | For 3000 ms after the rung goes false |
| `tp`, pulse | After the pulse, while the rung stays true | For 3000 ms after a rising edge: the pulse |
| `rto`, retentive | After the rung is true for a total of 3000 ms, until a reset | While the rung is true, `dn` is 0 and `r` is 0 |

Declare `t` with the [type](glossary.md#type) of the mnemonic: `var t ton` for `ton`. A
false rung sets `acc`, `dn` and `tt` of a `ton` to 0. An `rto` keeps `acc` and `dn` on a
false rung. A rung that writes 1 to `t.r` resets an `rto`. logex has no `res`
instruction.

### Rules of a timer

| Item | Rule |
|---|---|
| The preset | An integer literal: 0 to 2147483647 ms. A tag is an error. To set the preset from a tag, write the tag to `t.pre` with a `move` on an earlier rung. |
| A [long timer](glossary.md#long-timer) | Declare it with a long type: `var t ton_ltime`. The mnemonic stays `ton`. The preset and `acc` are in ns. The preset is 0 to 9223372036854775807. |
| The time of a long timer | It measures whole ms. A preset that is not a whole number of ms gives a warning. |
| The place on a rung | Last on its path. No instruction comes after it. |
| The result | Read `t.dn` on a different rung. |
| The instruction that runs it | Only 1 instruction can run a timer. A 2nd one is an error. |
| The time | The host gives each scan the time in ms since the last scan. A timer adds that time. It does not count scans. |
| A rung that a jump skips | The timer does not change. In its next run, it adds all the time since its last run. |
| `tp` on the first scan | It gives no pulse. |

### Members of a timer

| Member | Holds |
|---|---|
| `pre` | The [preset](glossary.md#preset) |
| `acc` | The time that the timer measures, in ms, or in ns for a long timer |
| `dn` | Done: the result |
| `tt` | Timing |
| `en` | The power in of the last run |
| `r` | Reset, only of `rto` and `rto_ltime` |

A rung can write `pre`, `acc` and `r`. It cannot write `dn`, `tt` or `en`.

[Instructions: timers and counters](instructions-timers-and-counters.md#timers) gives the
full rules.

## Counters

### `ctu`, `ctd` and `ctud`

<!-- unchecked: the forms of the 2 counter instructions, each with the preset 12 -->
```text
ctu c 12
ctd c 12
```

| Mnemonic | Each rising edge | Result |
|---|---|---|
| `ctu` | Adds 1 to `cv` | `q` is 1 when `cv` is `pv` or more |
| `ctd` | Subtracts 1 from `cv` | `q` is 1 when `cv` is 0 or less |

A `ctu` and a `ctd` can run 1 `ctud` counter. They must have the same preset. The
counter has `qu`, 1 when `cv` is `pv` or more, and `qd`, 1 when `cv` is 0 or less.

A rung that writes 1 to `c.r` sets `cv` to 0. A rung that writes 1 to `c.ld` sets `cv` to
`pv`. The counter does this in its next run. Rungs 3 and 4 of this program do it for
the `ctud` counter `stock`:

<!-- file: shelf.ld -->
```text
var_input eye_in bool
var_input eye_out bool
var_input clear bool
var_input fill bool
var stock ctud

xic eye_in ctu stock 40
xic eye_out ctd stock 40
xic clear ote stock.r
xic fill ote stock.ld
```

### Rules of a counter

| Item | Rule |
|---|---|
| The preset | An integer literal: -2147483648 to 2147483647. A tag is an error. To set the preset from a tag, write the tag to `c.pv` with a `move` on an earlier rung. |
| A [typed counter](glossary.md#typed-counter) | Declare it with a typed type: `var c ctu_int`. The mnemonic stays `ctu`. The preset is a value of the type of its count. |
| `cv` at the start | 0. Thus `q` of a `ctd` is 1 from the first scan, until `ld` loads `pv`. |
| The limit of the count | The count stops at the largest value of its type and sets `ov`. It stops at the smallest value and sets `un`. Each stays 1 until `r` or `ld` sets the count. |
| The place on a rung | Last on its path. No instruction comes after it. |
| The first scan | It does not count. |
| A rung that a jump skips | The counter does not change. In its next run, it compares its power in with the power in of its last run. |

### Members of a counter

| Member | Holds |
|---|---|
| `pv` | The preset |
| `cv` | The count |
| `q`, `qu`, `qd` | The result |
| `r` | Reset, not of `ctd` |
| `ld` | Load, not of `ctu` |
| `ov`, `un` | Overflow and underflow |
| `cu`, `cd` | The power in of the last run |

A rung can write `pv`, `r` and `ld`. It cannot write the other members.

[Instructions: timers and counters](instructions-timers-and-counters.md#counters) gives
the full rules.

## Move and arithmetic

### `move`

<!-- unchecked: the form of move -->
```text
move a d
```

| Rung | What `move` does |
|---|---|
| True | Writes the value of `a` to `d` |
| False | Writes nothing |

`a` has the type of `d`, or a type that [widens](glossary.md#widen) into it, for example
a `usint` into a `dint`. For each other type, use a conversion. A literal must fit `d`.
The mnemonic is `move`, not `mov`.

### Arithmetic

<!-- unchecked: the forms of the 8 arithmetic instructions -->
```text
add a b d
sub a b d
mul a b d
div a b d
mod a b d
neg a d
abs a d
sqrt a d
```

| Mnemonic | Writes to `d` |
|---|---|
| `add` | `a + b` |
| `sub` | `a - b` |
| `mul` | `a * b` |
| `div` | `a / b`. An integer quotient goes toward 0. |
| `mod` | The remainder of `a / b`, integers only. Its sign is the sign of `a`. |
| `neg` | `-a`. `d` is a signed type or a real. |
| `abs` | The absolute value of `a` |
| `sqrt` | The square root of `a`. `d` is a `real` or an `lreal`. |

| Mnemonic | Sources | Value in `d` |
|---|---|---|
| `sub` | 0 and 1, into a `usint` | 255 |
| `div` | -7 and 2 | -3 |
| `mod` | -7 and 3 | -1 |
| `neg` | -2.5 | 2.5 |
| `sqrt` | 16 | 4.0 |

Each source has the type of `d`, or a type that widens into it. On a true rung, each
instruction works in the type of `d`. It reads its sources before it writes, so
`add total flow total` is correct. On a false rung, each instruction writes nothing and
passes no power.

If each source is a literal, logex gives a warning: the result never changes. A `move`
of a literal gives no warning.

### Results that do not fit

| Case | Value in `d` | Power out |
|---|---|---|
| An integer result out of the range of `d` | It [wraps](glossary.md#wrap): 2147483647 + 1 is -2147483648 in a `dint` | False |
| A real result out of the range of `d` | The largest value of the type, with the sign | False |
| `div` or `mod` by 0 | 0, or 0.0 in a real | False |
| `sqrt` of a negative value | 0.0 | False |
| Each other result | The result. A real result is the nearest value of the type. | True |

`div` or `mod` by 0 gives the minor [fault](glossary.md#fault) `:divide_by_zero`. `sqrt`
of a negative value gives the minor fault `:negative_sqrt`. A wrap or a real result out
of the range gives no fault. A literal 0 divisor and a literal negative source of `sqrt`
are errors.

[Instructions: arithmetic and comparison](instructions-arithmetic-and-comparison.md)
gives the full rules.

## Comparisons
{: .col-2}

### `eq`, `ne`, `lt`, `gt`, `le` and `ge`

<!-- unchecked: the form of a comparison, with eq as the mnemonic -->
```text
eq a b
```

| Mnemonic | Passes power if |
|---|---|
| `eq` | `a = b` |
| `ne` | `a` is not equal to `b` |
| `lt` | `a < b` |
| `gt` | `a > b` |
| `le` | `a <= b` |
| `ge` | `a >= b` |

### Rules of a comparison

| Item | Rule |
|---|---|
| The operands | 2 numbers, or 2 bit strings |
| An integer and a real | Correct if the integer type widens into the real type: an `int` and a `real`. A `dint` and a `real` are an error. |
| 2 integer types | logex compares the values: a `sint` -1 is less than a `udint` 0. |
| A bit string and an integer tag | An error. Convert 1 of them first. |
| A bit string and an integer literal | Correct if the type holds the value: a `word` and `255` |
| A literal that the type of the other operand cannot hold | An error: a `usint` and `300` |
| A literal at the limit of the type | A warning if the result never changes: `ge` with a `usint` and `0` |
| 2 literals | A warning |
| A false rung | No power |

[Instructions: arithmetic and comparison](instructions-arithmetic-and-comparison.md#comparisons)
gives the full rules.

## Bitwise
{: .col-2}

### `and`, `or`, `xor` and `not`

<!-- unchecked: the forms of the 4 bitwise logic instructions -->
```text
and a b d
or a b d
xor a b d
not a d
```

| Mnemonic | Each bit of `d` |
|---|---|
| `and` | 1 if the bit is 1 in `a` and in `b` |
| `or` | 1 if the bit is 1 in `a` or in `b` |
| `xor` | 1 if the bit is 1 in only 1 of `a` and `b` |
| `not` | The opposite of the bit of `a` |

With `2#1100` and `2#1010`, `and` gives `2#1000`, `or` gives `2#1110` and `xor` gives
`2#0110`. `not` of the `byte` `2#0000_1111` is `2#1111_0000`. The operands are all bools
and bit strings, or all integers.

### `shl`, `shr`, `rol` and `ror`

<!-- unchecked: the forms of the 2 shifts and the 2 rotates -->
```text
shl a n d
shr a n d
rol a n d
ror a n d
```

| Mnemonic | What it does |
|---|---|
| `shl` | Moves the bits of `a` to the left by `n` places. It puts 0 in each empty bit. |
| `shr` | Moves the bits of `a` to the right by `n` places. It puts 0 in each empty bit. |
| `rol` | Rotates the bits of `a` to the left by `n` places |
| `ror` | Rotates the bits of `a` to the right by `n` places |

| Case | Result |
|---|---|
| The bits | A shift or a rotate uses each bit of the type of `a`, also the sign bit. `shr` of the `int` -2 by 1 gives 32767. |
| `n` equal to the width or more | A shift writes 0. A rotate uses the remainder of `n` divided by the width. |
| A tag `n` that is negative | It writes `a` with no shift and passes no power. |
| A literal negative `n` | An error |

On a false rung, a bitwise instruction writes nothing and passes no power. If each source
is a literal, logex gives a warning: the result never changes.
[Instructions: bitwise](instructions-bitwise.md) gives the full rules.

## Conversions

### The 4 groups

<!-- unchecked: the form of a conversion, with dint_to_sint as the mnemonic -->
```text
dint_to_sint a d
```

| Group | Number | Example |
|---|---|---|
| Integer | 56 | `dint_to_sint` |
| Bit string | 76 | `word_to_int` |
| Real | 34 | `real_to_int` |
| Truncation | 16 | `real_trunc_int` |

The mnemonic is the source type, `_to_` or `_trunc_`, and the destination type.

| Group | A value that does not fit |
|---|---|
| Integer | It wraps: 129 is -127 in a `sint`. |
| Bit string | It keeps the low bits. |
| Real | Into an integer: the nearest integer, wrapped. Into a `real`: the largest value, with the sign. |
| Truncation | The integer part, wrapped |

A bit string conversion passes its power in. The other conversions pass no power if the
value does not fit.

### Values

| Conversion | Result |
|---|---|
| `real_to_int` of 2.5 | 2 |
| `real_to_int` of 3.5 | 4 |
| `real_trunc_int` of -1.6 | -1 |
| `int_to_dword` of -1 | `16#0000ffff` |
| `word_to_int` of `16#ffff` | -1 |
| `dint_to_real` of 16777217 | 16777216.0 |

A tie goes to the even integer: 2.5 gives 2 and 3.5 gives 4. On a false rung, a
conversion writes nothing and passes no power. A bool has no conversion. A literal
source gives a warning. [Conversions](conversions.md) gives the full rules and the 182
mnemonics.

## Jump and call

### `jmp`, `lbl` and `cal`

> #### Warning {: .warning}
>
> Do not put the stop of a machine only on a rung that a `jmp` can skip. A rung that the
> scan does not run writes nothing. An [output](glossary.md#output) that is 1 stays 1.

<!-- unchecked: the forms of jmp, lbl and cal, with 1 var_input and 1 var_output in the block of f -->
```text
jmp l
lbl l
cal f a d
```

| Mnemonic | True rung | False rung |
|---|---|---|
| `jmp` | The rest of its rung runs. Then the scan does not run the rungs between this rung and the rung of the label `l`. | It jumps nowhere. |
| `lbl` | Gives the [label](glossary.md#label) `l` to its rung | The same |
| `cal` | Copies `a` into the `var_input` tag of the instance `f`, runs the rungs of `f` and copies its `var_output` tag to `d` | Copies nothing and runs nothing |

### Rules of a jump and a call

| Item | Rule |
|---|---|
| A jump | Forward only, to a later rung of the same [routine](glossary.md#routine). 1 `jmp` on a rung. |
| `lbl` | First on its rung. A label that no `jmp` names is a warning. |
| The instruction that runs an instance | Only 1 `cal` can run an instance. A 2nd one is an error. |
| A rung that the scan does not run | Each tag keeps its value. Timers, counters and one-shots do not change. In its next run, a timer adds all the time since its last run. |
| The operands of `cal` | The instance, then a value for each `var_input`, then a tag for each `var_output` of the [function block](glossary.md#function-block). Each group is in the sequence of its [declarations](glossary.md#declaration). |
| Power out | The power in, for the 3 instructions |

[Instructions: jump and call](instructions-jump-and-call.md) gives the full rules.

## Power out and faults
{: .col-2}

### What comes after an instruction

| Instruction | Power out |
|---|---|
| A contact, a comparison | Its result |
| A coil, `osr`, `osf`, `move`, `jmp`, `lbl`, `cal` | Its power in |
| `ons` | True for 1 scan at a rising edge |
| An arithmetic instruction, an integer conversion, a real conversion, a truncation | Its power in, but false if the value does not fit or after a fault |
| A bit string conversion | Its power in |
| `and`, `or`, `xor`, `not` | Its power in |
| A shift or a rotate | Its power in, but false for a negative tag `n` |
| A timer or a counter instruction | No instruction can come after it. |

On a false rung, the power out of each instruction is false.

### Faults

| Fault | Kind and cause |
|---|---|
| `:divide_by_zero` | [Minor](glossary.md#minor-fault): `div` or `mod` by 0 |
| `:negative_sqrt` | Minor: `sqrt` of a negative value |
| `:index_out_of_range` | [Major](glossary.md#major-fault): a tag subscript outside the array |
| `:bit_out_of_range` | Major: a tag bit number outside the type |

An instruction with a fault passes no power. The scan runs the next instruction.
[Faults](faults.md) gives what logex does with each fault.

